219
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:13,200
On one hand, when you look at Shenzhen, it's a very dense ecosystem.

220
00:19:13,200 --> 00:19:14,960
You have a lot of different forces.

221
00:19:14,960 --> 00:19:17,359
You have very smart policy reforms.

222
00:19:17,359 --> 00:19:18,859
You have multinational companies.

223
00:19:18,799 --> 00:19:20,799
You have many entrepreneurs.

224
00:19:20,799 --> 00:19:22,399
You have vibrant universities.

225
00:19:22,399 --> 00:19:24,119
You have talent flowing in.

226
00:19:24,119 --> 00:19:26,119
You have a neighborhood to Hong Kong.

227
00:19:26,119 --> 00:19:30,159
But then you also have openness where you get, it's attracting a lot of people because

228
00:19:30,159 --> 00:19:33,959
it has made such good advancement for technology.

229
00:19:33,959 --> 00:19:37,399
That creates opportunities for knowledge spillover.

230
00:19:37,399 --> 00:19:43,519
You have a collaboration economy where you really have the dynamism that in a way makes that happen.

231
00:19:43,519 --> 00:19:50,519
I believe what Fabian said earlier, in a way we can't replicate Shenzhen that easily,

232
00:19:50,519 --> 00:19:52,759
but we can learn from the logic.

233
00:19:52,759 --> 00:19:55,279
The logic is about link and learn.

234
00:19:55,279 --> 00:19:58,799
You have in a way players who are mutually creating value.

235
00:19:58,799 --> 00:20:00,399
They recombine knowledge.

236
00:20:00,399 --> 00:20:04,480
There will be knowledge spillover across different actors and industries.

237
00:20:04,480 --> 00:20:07,519
That makes it so successful.

238
00:20:07,519 --> 00:20:14,559
Mr. Zhou, what do you think of the kind of inspirations that Shenzhen can give?

239
00:20:14,559 --> 00:20:22,440
Is Shenzhen a really isolated case or a very much product of China's active top-down approach,

240
00:20:22,440 --> 00:20:30,279
but also allowing the market to be the decisive force of where the economy is, where resources

241
00:20:30,279 --> 00:20:32,519
are allocated?

242
00:20:32,519 --> 00:20:37,559
How does the story Shenzhen reflect the broader growth story of China?

243
00:20:37,559 --> 00:20:44,880
Well, Shenzhen is really our place to try to find something that other places haven't

244
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:46,380
been doing.

245
00:20:45,960 --> 00:20:51,120
So it's kind of a creation, some new space, some new channels for competition.

246
00:20:51,119 --> 00:20:54,799
So it's not just trying to maintain its position as before.

247
00:20:54,799 --> 00:20:59,319
Well, when I'm discussing about the technology, about the talents, I think that it also needs

248
00:20:59,319 --> 00:21:05,119
to know that the manufacturing basis of Guangdong, Hong Kong, and Macau, these great big areas,

249
00:21:05,119 --> 00:21:10,679
is very important for Shenzhen to realize its abilities from the technology, from the laboratories,

250
00:21:10,679 --> 00:21:12,359
into the market practitioners.

251
00:21:12,359 --> 00:21:18,319
So these are what they are really giving the world, so many opportunities and so many successful stories.

252
00:21:18,319 --> 00:21:24,799
You just mentioned the keyword competition and you actually hear a lot of talk about

253
00:21:24,799 --> 00:21:27,240
how competitive the environment is.

254
00:21:27,240 --> 00:21:29,839
And if you look at the numbers, it's very scary.

255
00:21:29,839 --> 00:21:39,799
For instance, for the city of Shenzhen, it has 2,600 AI enterprises and over 1,000 core

256
00:21:39,799 --> 00:21:42,799
robotics companies.

257
00:21:42,799 --> 00:21:49,839
And there are many, many more I can name, but I think these two numbers are already very telling.

258
00:21:49,839 --> 00:21:56,960
Is it possible for any company to survive in this kind of environment and still stand out?

259
00:21:56,960 --> 00:21:59,200
And Fabian, I want to go to...

260
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:06,519
Is it not like a shark tank in a way for enterprises and for investors, for entrepreneurs?

261
00:22:06,519 --> 00:22:09,039
No, no, I don't think so.

262
00:22:09,039 --> 00:22:14,799
I think it's great because we need competition, you need friction.

263
00:22:14,799 --> 00:22:19,399
It's from the friction that people can try to do their best.

264
00:22:19,399 --> 00:22:27,319
And this also can let some appear, some great new practices, management practices, best

265
00:22:27,319 --> 00:22:28,819
practice.

266
00:22:28,319 --> 00:22:35,000
So it's through competition and also economic cooperation that it's possible to see some

267
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,279
great things coming.

268
00:22:37,279 --> 00:22:44,799
And Professor Fiedler, what is your take on the ecosystem and the kind of fears, competitiveness

269
00:22:44,799 --> 00:22:48,759
that I just mentioned everybody is talking about?

270
00:22:48,759 --> 00:22:56,000
Is there anything that can be learned from there in your mind or is something that is probably too much?

271
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:01,599
For my research, what I know is that often entrepreneurs, they leverage knowledge and

272
00:23:01,599 --> 00:23:09,279
talent that is based within Shenzhen, but they also leverage their own home country advantages and global linkages.

273
00:23:09,279 --> 00:23:13,559
So what entrepreneurs would do, they maybe would build their business in Shenzhen, but

274
00:23:13,559 --> 00:23:18,759
they often would then export technology from Shenzhen and markets such as Africa, and it

275
00:23:18,759 --> 00:23:21,240
provides a solution for home country.

276
00:23:21,240 --> 00:23:26,480
And this unique combination makes it very hard for competitors to exactly copy the same

277
00:23:26,480 --> 00:23:31,919
business model because the entrepreneurs also have their own knowledge about foreign markets,

278
00:23:31,919 --> 00:23:36,039
for example, that is hard to just replicate.

279
00:23:36,079 --> 00:23:42,639
So this gives distinct combinations of ecosystems where you leverage Shenzhen to other places

280
00:23:42,639 --> 00:23:45,440
that is not so easily to copy.

281
00:23:45,440 --> 00:23:52,639
So really there is an opportunity really to create global companies using Shenzhen as a hub.

282
00:23:52,639 --> 00:23:57,559
Yeah, Mr. Jomi, how do you look at the kind of integration that has been taking place

283
00:23:57,559 --> 00:24:04,119
behind the growth of Shenzhen as a city, but also the regional supply chains and foreign investors here?

284
00:24:04,559 --> 00:24:06,719
Well, I think that there are two forces.

285
00:24:06,719 --> 00:24:11,559
Shenzhen is leading the ways of innovation and they require some of the manufacturing

286
00:24:11,559 --> 00:24:15,959
abilities which is supported by the supply chains beside it.

287
00:24:15,959 --> 00:24:19,159
And also another force is coming from the manufacturing themselves.

288
00:24:19,159 --> 00:24:26,679
They want to upgrade, they want to change, they want to have a better optimization of the using of the resources.

289
00:24:26,679 --> 00:24:33,559
So these two forces are coming to Shenzhen to integrate there and they attracted so many talents to go to that place.

290
00:24:33,599 --> 00:24:35,599
So this is a kind of integration.

291
00:24:35,599 --> 00:24:39,119
And the momentum is spiking as I said over the past few years.

292
00:24:39,119 --> 00:24:41,000
Speed has really been picking up.

293
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:46,759
If you look at the BOID for instance, it has already surpassed Tesla as the world's largest

294
00:24:46,759 --> 00:24:51,799
EV maker and it's eyeing the position as the world's biggest automaker.

295
00:24:51,799 --> 00:24:56,399
Where's the limit for companies such as BOID and for cities such as Shenzhen?

296
00:24:56,399 --> 00:24:58,839
Is it a bubble, Mr. Joe?

297
00:24:58,839 --> 00:25:00,639
I don't think that there is a bubble.

298
00:25:00,640 --> 00:25:05,880
They are really creating more demand and if you're looking at demand or the voices from

299
00:25:05,880 --> 00:25:11,160
many countries, they really want to import more electrical vehicles from China.

300
00:25:11,160 --> 00:25:17,120
I think they really want to have that because they have promised to have a better and green development.

301
00:25:17,120 --> 00:25:20,720
So they need to transfer from the fossil fuels into the new energies.

302
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:28,240
Fabienne, what is your take when you look at the kind of speed and scale of the growth in Shenzhen?

303
00:25:28,240 --> 00:25:30,120
What is the biggest challenge then?

304
00:25:31,119 --> 00:25:32,619
For sure.

305
00:25:32,119 --> 00:25:35,319
They need to keep the pace.

306
00:25:35,319 --> 00:25:40,959
They need to continue to, you know, there is such level of innovation already.

307
00:25:40,959 --> 00:25:42,839
But I'm very confident.

308
00:25:42,839 --> 00:25:48,039
I'm very positive because when we see what's happening concerning the advanced material,

309
00:25:48,039 --> 00:25:53,079
advanced manufacturing, future industries, new biotech companies in Shenzhen, we know

310
00:25:53,159 --> 00:26:05,159
that this ecosystem is here to stay because it's here to keep a certain activity because

311
00:26:05,159 --> 00:26:11,240
it's so rich and so abundant nowadays that with, especially with new technology like

312
00:26:11,240 --> 00:26:18,480
AI and robots, we know that it's not going to disappear as fast as it's arrived.

313
00:26:18,480 --> 00:26:22,640
And China is now a solution provider, a solution exporter.

314
00:26:22,640 --> 00:26:25,839
And this is also very transforming something.

315
00:26:25,839 --> 00:26:30,120
You know, it's a transformation benefit to other countries.

316
00:26:30,120 --> 00:26:31,759
It's very exciting.

317
00:26:31,759 --> 00:26:39,000
Let me do the job of my Western mainstream media colleagues and ask the question, at what cost?

318
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:42,120
Fabienne, I'm directing this question to you.

319
00:26:42,120 --> 00:26:45,680
The speed of Shenzhen, the growth of Shenzhen, at what cost?

320
00:26:45,680 --> 00:26:47,680
Is it a sustainable growth?

321
00:26:48,680 --> 00:26:53,839
I think it's sustainable because it's, you know, Shenzhen is very green nowadays.

322
00:26:53,839 --> 00:26:56,319
Shenzhen is full of clean energies.

323
00:26:56,319 --> 00:27:00,880
Shenzhen respect the UNSGD, the sustainable development goals.

324
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:07,680
And there is a special system orchestration and a full of efficiency for energy consumption.

325
00:27:07,680 --> 00:27:11,680
And I think we can say that this ecosystem is sustainable.

326
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:17,599
And finally, Professor Fidler, on top of learning from Shenzhen from China and I want to go

327
00:27:17,599 --> 00:27:23,119
to our guest in New Zealand on top of learning from cities such as in China, what are you

328
00:27:23,119 --> 00:27:25,159
looking for in other parts of the world?

329
00:27:25,159 --> 00:27:31,119
And how, you know, what are the pieces in your map for the future that you want to show to your students?

330
00:27:33,319 --> 00:27:37,919
I take students since more than a decade to different cities in Asia.

331
00:27:37,919 --> 00:27:41,119
We also visited, for example, Shanghai in the past.

332
00:27:41,119 --> 00:27:46,439
I believe for me, Asia is a really hot spot as a larger region because it does complement

333
00:27:46,440 --> 00:27:50,960
really the knowledge and the products and services we offer here.

334
00:27:50,960 --> 00:27:54,279
So there's this complementary knowledge and skills.

335
00:27:54,279 --> 00:27:56,279
So for me, Asia is the future.

336
00:27:56,279 --> 00:28:00,799
And I think there's a lot to learn that the best can really look to Asia and learn from Asia.

337
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,960
All right. Thank you so much for this interesting discussion.

338
00:28:04,960 --> 00:28:10,279
We tried to scratch the surface, but the city of Shenzhen is fascinating.

339
00:28:10,279 --> 00:28:15,080
I've been there a couple of times, but I'm yet to understand the real, you know, gist

340
00:28:15,119 --> 00:28:20,000
of the story. Hopefully we'll continue to dig further into their subject.

341
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:25,240
Many thanks to my guest, Auntie Fidler, assistant professor at the University of Auckland and

342
00:28:25,240 --> 00:28:31,439
director of the New Zealand Asia Institute from Shenzhen, Fabian Pacheri, executive vice

343
00:28:31,439 --> 00:28:33,720
president of CCI France, Sheen.

344
00:28:34,039 --> 00:28:39,159
And here in the studio, Joe Me, deputy director of the Institute of American and Oceania Studies

345
00:28:39,159 --> 00:28:43,000
at the Chinese Academy of International Trade and Economic Corporation.

346
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:49,640
And with that, we come to the end of this edition of The Point with me, Lu Xin, coming to you from Beijing.

347
00:28:49,640 --> 00:28:53,880
As always, you can follow us on Facebook and X using the handle Lu Xin in Beijing on behalf

348
00:28:53,880 --> 00:28:56,640
of the whole team. Thank you for watching. You've got the point.
